{"qas": [
  {"question": "According to the OECD Recommendation on AI, how did the 2023 and 2024 revisions address generative AI and information integrity?", "answer": "The 2023 revision clarified the definition of 'AI System' to include generative AI, while the 2024 revision emphasized addressing misinformation and information integrity.", "evidence": [{"page": 4, "quote": "The update of the definition included edits aimed at:\n\n- clarifying that the Recommendation applies to generative AI systems, which produce \"content\";"}, {"page": 4, "quote": "- reflecting the growing importance of addressing misinformation and disinformation, and safeguarding information integrity in the context of generative AI;"}]},
  {"question": "What are the five high-level values-based principles and five national policy recommendations in the OECD Recommendation on AI?", "answer": "The five principles are: inclusive growth, respect for law and rights, transparency, robustness, and accountability. The five recommendations are: investing in R&D, fostering an AI ecosystem, enabling governance, building human capacity, and international cooperation.", "evidence": [{"page": 4, "quote": "The Recommendation contains five high-level values-based principles and five recommendations for national policies and international co-operation."}, {"page": 4, "quote": "1. Principles for responsible stewardship of trustworthy AI: the first section sets out five complementary principles relevant to all stakeholders: i) inclusive growth, sustainable development and well-being; ii) respect for the rule of law, human rights and democratic values, including fairness and privacy; iii) transparency and explainability; iv) robustness, security and safety; and v) accountability. ... 2. National policies and international co-operation for trustworthy AI: ... i) investing in AI research and development; ii) fostering an inclusive AI-enabling ecosystem; iii) shaping an enabling interoperable governance and policy environment for AI; iv) building human capacity and preparing for labour market transformation; and v) international co-operation for trustworthy AI."}]},
  {"question": "In the OECD Recommendation on AI, what does 'AI system lifecycle' mean?", "answer": "The 'AI system lifecycle' refers to the phases from planning and design, through deployment and monitoring, to retirement or decommissioning, often in an iterative manner.", "evidence": [{"page": 8, "quote": "- AI system lifecycle: An AI system lifecycle typically involves several phases that include to: plan and design; collect and process data; build model(s) and/or adapt existing model(s) to specific tasks; test, evaluate, verify and validate; make available for use/deploy; operate and monitor; and retire/decommission. These phases often take place in an iterative manner and are not necessarily sequential. The decision to retire an AI system from operation may occur at any point during the operation and monitoring phase."}]},
  {"question": "What is the difference between an OECD Recommendation and an OECD Decision, as described in the OECD Recommendation on AI?", "answer": "An OECD Decision is legally binding on Members (except abstainers), while an OECD Recommendation is not legally binding but represents a political commitment.", "evidence": [{"page": 12, "quote": "· Decisions are adopted by Council and are legally binding on all Members except those which abstain at the time of adoption. ... · Recommendations are adopted by Council and are not legally binding. They represent a political commitment to the principles they contain and entail an expectation that Adherents will do their best to implement them."}]},
  {"question": "Which group was established by the DPC in 2018 to help develop the OECD Recommendation on AI, and what was its composition?", "answer": "The informal AI Group of experts at the OECD was established, comprising over 50 experts from government, industry, civil society, trade unions, the technical community, and academia.", "evidence": [{"page": 3, "quote": "The informal AI Group of experts at the OECD was subsequently established, comprising over 50 experts from different disciplines and different sectors (government, industry, civil society, trade unions, the technical community and academia)"}]},
  {"question": "How does the OECD Recommendation on AI describe the responsibility of AI actors for the functioning of AI systems?", "answer": "AI actors are accountable for proper functioning and respecting principles, ensuring traceability and applying systematic risk management throughout the AI system lifecycle.", "evidence": [{"page": 9, "quote": "a) AI actors should be accountable for the proper functioning of AI systems and for the respect of the above principles, based on their roles, the context, and consistent with the state of the art."}]}
]}
